Z||000000||speed racer||06-11-2001||12:06 AM||||Can some one tell me the best way to paint the break calipers im getting new wheels and you will be able to see them. And also what color do you guys think they should they be red or black I have a red 86 SE||||reg||1
Z||000001||AkursedX||06-11-2001||12:13 AM||||Just get some regular high temp engine paint or something of the sort.  I painted mine silver over a year ago and they still look great. <P>Oh, and I would paint them black before red.  Just my 2-cents<P>EDIT:  Oh, and I sandblasted and primed them before i painted them.<p>[This message has been edited by AkursedX (edited 06-11-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000002||AusFiero||06-11-2001||07:49 AM||||There is actually a brake caliper paint available. Different stuff to the engine paint.<P>Colour really depends on your car. I have red I am yet to use but mine is a red car also.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/fiero5.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A><BR>Need a Subwoofer Console or Speaker Door Pods?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Alex4mula||06-11-2001||09:45 AM||||I used a new caliper paint sold at Pepboys. Clean them very well with break cleaner and then soap and water. I painted mines on the car. Easy and results were excellent. Four months now and still look like new. Red looks very nice on red cars. I put a black Fiero decal also.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.zing.com/picture/p1e80fe805bf3773b054078ff55c24d33/fe690910.jpg.orig.jpg">  <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.zing.com/picture/p45677fc1b1c241a1edf7d38cfba150ec/fe6908be.jpg.orig.jpg">  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Galen||06-11-2001||12:49 PM||||I know I'm always posting something that doesn't hold to the thread but anyways.. sweet rims  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||Alex4mula||06-11-2001||01:15 PM||||Thanks. I understand what you mean. Those ride every day  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.||||reg||1
Z||000006||1FST2M6||06-11-2001||01:24 PM||||  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1fstcaliper1.jpg">   <P>Caliper paint just thicker and usually contains ceramic chips as a bonding agent and heat dispersion.. some engine paints contain the same stuff...  mine were new when i shot them.. but just tape over the piston, bleed screw and slides and shoot away...  after cleaning it with simple green or brake parts cleaner (spray can)  <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1fstcaliper2.jpg">   <P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 06-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||rogergarrison||06-11-2001||04:58 PM||||I just paint them with regular automotive paint. Last for years on mine (red too). Main thing is getting them clean first. I dont really think that heat is a problem unless your very aggressive with brakes.At least, I havent had a problem. BTW, has anyone seen anyone selling chrome plated ones, I think those would be nice.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 06-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||88formula||06-11-2001||05:07 PM||||I used hi-temp Dupli-color aluminum DE1615 from autozone to paint mine. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Rear_Brake_calipers_resized_2.JPG"> <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||hyhy4u||06-12-2001||04:11 PM||||I just used reg. plain old spray paint from the hardware store, is holding up just fine, i got a Met. Red Fiero and i used dark red and it looks sweet.||||reg||10
Z||000010||Jay||06-12-2001||06:36 PM||||I used high heat paint (red) and applied it on the car with a paint brush.  Then after a about a half hour I went and drove around to heat up the caliper.  The paint is nice and smooth and has lasted about 3 years.  Be careful not to bond the slider to the caliper when doing this.<P>------------------<BR>Jay<BR>1986 Northstar GT<BR>www.members.home.net/jaygrande<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/kick.gif">  <BR>THE NORTHSTAR GIVES YOU A REAL KICK IN THE ASS!!!||||reg||1
Z||000011||Seanh||06-12-2001||07:03 PM||||I tried doing this on my 86 when I rebuilt the rear calipers. I primed them, painted them, and clear coated them, all with high temp engine paint. They looked great. I was installing them and got a little brake fluid on them, wiped it off, and the paint came right off. Brake fluid just seems to dissolve the paint right off it. Now, In a mag. for Porsche performance I was ordering from, has a caliper paint kit. It uses Lacquar and includes enough to do 4 calipers, but it cost $50! Think I should get it for the fiero? For some reason, paint just doesn't like me. ||||reg||1
Z||000012||isthiswhereiputausername?||06-12-2001||10:39 PM||||I sprayed mine with chrome paint.. then clearcoated them..  I think they look great  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||1
